      "static_evidence": [
        "The unsigned GUI PE installs an extension-concealing wrapper for EXE launches and contains an intact HTTP cache-download-to-WinExec chain. This combination establishes an unwanted loader, not just association maintenance or a downloader.",
        "0x4063c0 builds the exefile open-command handler; 0x4064ff/0x406512 call strrchr for a dot, and 0x406517 truncates the copied wrapper filename. 0x40651a appends the original executable/argument template before the registry write helper at 0x404610.",
        "0x4052b3 resolves URLDownloadToCacheFileA using GetProcAddress. The ESI call at 0x4052f3 fills a stack pathname. The branch at 0x405307 reaches the same-path extension-check and WinExec at 0x4053d1. All three stack LEAs resolve to one pathname.",
        "The original file has exactly zero bytes at file offsets 0x3000..0x3fff and an empty .PEC section. POP3 USER/PASS/UIDL/RETR handling survives in part, but the stronger nine-byte X-tag mailbox loader signature does not. No such signature was loosened to recover a score.",
        "Purpose/deployment: no signed vendor or repair context is available. Concealing the wrapper extension, interposing on all EXE launches, and loading network content tip the verdict. Runtime success and downloaded payload identity are unproven."
      ],
